  '["AWS is solely responsible for all customer data encryption.","The customer is always responsible for encrypting their data in transit and at rest.","AWS is responsible for encrypting data at rest, and the customer for data in transit.","The shared responsibility model does not apply to data security."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the customer''s responsibility for data. Under the Shared Responsibility Model, customers are always responsible for their data, including its encryption, integrity, and access management, both in transit and at rest. While AWS provides services like AWS Key Management Service (KMS) to facilitate encryption, the decision to use them and how to configure them rests with the customer. Option A is incorrect as AWS does not solely handle all customer data encryption. Option C incorrectly divides the responsibility for encryption. Option D is incorrect as data security is a core component of the shared responsibility model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_42',
  'A development team is running an application on Amazon EC2 instances. Which of the following is a customer responsibility according to the AWS Shared Responsibility Model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Maintaining the physical security of the AWS data centers.","Ensuring the availability of the AWS global infrastructure.","Configuring security groups and network ACLs for the EC2 instances.","Patching the virtualization software on the host servers."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is a customer responsibility. For Amazon EC2, which is an IaaS offering, customers are responsible for configuring their network security, including security groups and network ACLs, to control traffic to and from their instances. Options A, B, and D are all responsibilities of AWS as part of the security ''of'' the cloud.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_43',
  'Which of the following is an AWS responsibility under the Shared Responsibility Model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Application-level security","Operating system patching on Amazon EC2","Configuration of IAM policies","Security of the underlying hardware and global infrastructure"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is an AWS responsibility. AWS is responsible for the security ''of'' the cloud, which includes the underlying hardware, software, networking, and facilities that run AWS services. Options A, B, and C are all customer responsibilities for security ''in'' the cloud.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_44',
  'A company is deploying a new web application on AWS. They need to ensure that their application code is secure. Whose responsibility is it to secure the application code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS, as part of security ''of'' the cloud.","The customer, as part of security ''in'' the cloud.","A third-party auditor, as per compliance requirements.","The responsibility is shared equally between AWS and the customer."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the customer''s responsibility. Securing application code is part of the customer''s responsibility for security ''in'' the cloud. This includes writing secure code, performing code reviews, and managing application-level vulnerabilities. Option A is incorrect because AWS secures the underlying infrastructure, not customer applications. Option C is incorrect as auditors verify compliance, they don''t secure the code. Option D is incorrect because this specific aspect falls squarely on the customer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_45',
  'When using Amazon RDS, a managed database service, which security task is AWS responsible for?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing database user accounts and permissions.","Patching the database engine software.","Encrypting data within the database tables.","Configuring network access rules for the database."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is an AWS responsibility. For managed services like Amazon RDS, AWS manages the underlying infrastructure, operating system, and the database engine software, including patching and updates. Options A, C, and D are customer responsibilities. Customers are responsible for managing database users, encrypting their data, and configuring security groups/network ACLs for database access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_46',
  'A customer is concerned about unauthorized access to their AWS account. Which of the following is a customer responsibility to mitigate this risk?',
  'mcq',
  '["Ensuring the physical security of AWS data centers.","Providing Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for their IAM users.","Patching the underlying virtualization software.","Maintaining the global network infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is a customer responsibility. Customers are responsible for managing access to their AWS accounts and resources, which includes enabling and enforcing Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) for IAM users to enhance security. Options A, C, and D are all responsibilities of AWS under the Shared Responsibility Model.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_47',
  'What is the primary distinction between AWS''s responsibility and the customer''s responsibility for security under the Shared Responsibility Model?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS is responsible for security ''in'' the cloud, while the customer is responsible for security ''of'' the cloud.","AWS is responsible for security ''of'' the cloud, while the customer is responsible for security ''in'' the cloud.","AWS is responsible for physical security only, and the customer for all software security.","The customer is responsible for all security aspects, and AWS provides tools."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the primary distinction. AWS is responsible for the security ''of'' the cloud, which means protecting the infrastructure that runs all AWS services. The customer is responsible for security ''in'' the cloud, which includes securing their data, applications, operating systems, and network configurations within the AWS environment. Option A reverses these responsibilities. Option C is too narrow, as AWS also manages software like the hypervisor. Option D is incorrect as AWS has significant security responsibilities.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_48',
  'A company is using Amazon S3 to store critical business documents. Which of the following is a customer responsibility regarding the security of these documents?',
  'mcq',
  '["Ensuring the durability of S3 storage.","Configuring the S3 bucket policies to control access.","Patching the underlying S3 infrastructure.","Maintaining the availability of the S3 service."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is a customer responsibility. Customers are responsible for configuring access controls, such as S3 bucket policies and IAM policies, to ensure only authorized users can access their documents stored in Amazon S3. Options A, C, and D are all responsibilities of AWS as part of the security ''of'' the cloud for a managed service like Amazon S3.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_49',
  'In the AWS shared responsibility model, what is the customer primarily responsible for when deploying an application on Amazon EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["The physical security of the data centers.","The security of the underlying hypervisor.","Configuring operating system patches and application-level security.","Maintaining the global network infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is configuring operating system patches and application-level security. For IaaS services like Amazon EC2, the customer is responsible for ''security IN the cloud'', which includes managing the guest operating system (including updates and security patches), application software, and the configuration of security groups and network ACLs. Option A (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility. Option B (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility. Option D (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_50',
  'A customer is using Amazon RDS for their relational database. According to the AWS shared responsibility model, which of the following is an AWS responsibility for this service?',
  'mcq',
  '["Managing database user accounts and permissions.","Patching the underlying operating system and database engine.","Configuring database backups and recovery procedures.","Encrypting data within the database tables."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is patching the underlying operating system and database engine. For managed services like Amazon RDS, AWS takes on more responsibility compared to IaaS. AWS is responsible for the security of the cloud, which includes the underlying infrastructure, OS patching, and database engine patching. Option A (incorrect) is a customer responsibility, as customers manage their database users and their permissions. Option C (incorrect) is a customer responsibility, although RDS provides tools to facilitate this. Option D (incorrect) is a customer responsibility (ap-2.1-001), as customers are responsible for encrypting their data, even if AWS provides the encryption features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_51',
  'Which statement best describes the ''security OF the cloud'' responsibility in the AWS shared responsibility model?',
  'mcq',
  '["The customer''s responsibility for securing their applications and data.","AWS''s responsibility for protecting the infrastructure that runs all of the services offered in the AWS Cloud.","The customer''s responsibility for configuring network access controls.","AWS''s responsibility for managing customer data encryption."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS''s responsibility for protecting the infrastructure that runs all of the services offered in the AWS Cloud. ''Security OF the cloud'' refers to AWS''s responsibility for the physical infrastructure, hypervisor, global network, and the foundational services. Option A (incorrect) describes ''security IN the cloud'', which is the customer''s responsibility. Option C (incorrect) describes ''security IN the cloud'', which is the customer''s responsibility (ap-2.1-004). Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-001) as managing customer data encryption is generally a customer responsibility, even if AWS provides the tools (like KMS).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_52',
  'A customer is storing highly sensitive data in Amazon S3. According to the AWS shared responsibility model, who is responsible for encrypting this data at rest within the S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS is fully responsible for encrypting all data stored in Amazon S3.","The customer is responsible for configuring data encryption for their data in Amazon S3.","A third-party auditor is responsible for ensuring data encryption.","AWS and the customer share responsibility equally, with AWS performing the encryption and the customer providing the keys."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that the customer is responsible for configuring data encryption for their data in Amazon S3. This falls under ''security IN the cloud''. While AWS provides the encryption features (like S3-managed keys, KMS-managed keys, or customer-provided keys), the customer must choose and configure the appropriate encryption method. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-001) as AWS does not automatically encrypt all customer data without customer configuration. Option C (incorrect) is incorrect; auditors review compliance but are not responsible for implementing security controls. Option D (incorrect) is partially true for KMS, but the ultimate responsibility for *configuring* the encryption remains with the customer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_53',
  'When using AWS Lambda, which aspect of security is AWS responsible for?',
  'mcq',
  '["Securing the application code deployed to AWS Lambda.","Managing the underlying compute infrastructure and operating system patching.","Configuring IAM permissions for the Lambda function to access other AWS services.","Ensuring the data processed by the Lambda function is validated and sanitized."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is managing the underlying compute infrastructure and operating system patching. For serverless services like AWS Lambda, AWS takes on a significant portion of the ''security OF the cloud'' responsibility, including the underlying compute, storage, network, and the operating system that runs the Lambda function. Option A (incorrect) is a customer responsibility, as the customer writes and deploys the application code. Option C (incorrect) is a customer responsibility, as customers configure IAM roles for their Lambda functions. Option D (incorrect) is a customer responsibility, as application-level data validation and sanitization are part of the customer''s application logic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_54',
  'A company has deployed a web server on an Amazon EC2 instance. They need to ensure that only HTTP and HTTPS traffic can reach the web server from the internet. Who is responsible for configuring the network firewall rules to allow this specific traffic?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS, as part of ''security OF the cloud''.","The customer, by configuring Security Groups and Network ACLs.","AWS Support, upon request from the customer.","The internet service provider, before traffic reaches AWS."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the customer, by configuring Security Groups and Network ACLs. This falls under ''security IN the cloud''. Customers are responsible for configuring their network security controls, such as Security Groups (instance-level firewall) and Network ACLs (subnet-level firewall), to control inbound and outbound traffic to their Amazon EC2 instances. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-004) as AWS provides the tools but the customer must configure them. Option C (incorrect) is not how AWS Support operates; customers are expected to configure their own resources. Option D (incorrect) is incorrect; the ISP handles general internet connectivity, not specific firewall rules within the customer''s AWS VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_55',
  'Which of the following is an example of a customer responsibility under the AWS shared responsibility model for an Amazon S3 bucket?',
  'mcq',
  '["Maintaining the physical hardware where S3 data is stored.","Ensuring the durability and availability of the S3 service.","Configuring bucket policies and access control lists (ACLs) to control who can access the data.","Patching the operating system of the underlying S3 infrastructure."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is configuring bucket policies and access control lists (ACLs) to control who can access the data. This is a key aspect of ''security IN the cloud'' for Amazon S3. Customers are responsible for managing access permissions to their S3 buckets and objects. Option A (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility. Option B (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility, as S3 is designed for high durability and availability. Option D (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility, as S3 is a managed service and AWS handles the underlying infrastructure patching.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_56',
  'A company is running a legacy application on an Amazon EC2 instance that requires a specific version of an operating system. Who is responsible for applying security patches and updates to this operating system?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS, as part of the managed service offering.","The customer, as part of ''security IN the cloud''.","AWS Support, if a critical vulnerability is discovered.","The operating system vendor, directly to the EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the customer, as part of ''security IN the cloud''. For Amazon EC2, which is an Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) offering, the customer is responsible for managing the guest operating system, including all security patches and updates. This is a common misconception (ap-2.1-002) where customers might assume AWS handles all patching. Option A (incorrect) would be true for managed services like Amazon RDS or AWS Lambda, but not for EC2''s guest OS. Option C (incorrect) is not how AWS Support works for OS patching. Option D (incorrect) is not how patching is typically managed in a cloud environment; the customer is responsible for applying patches within their OS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_57',
  'Which of the following is an example of an AWS responsibility under the shared responsibility model?',
  'mcq',
  '["Customer data encryption for Amazon S3.","Configuration of Security Groups and Network ACLs.","Physical security of AWS data centers.","Operating system patching for Amazon EC2 instances."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the physical security of AWS data centers. This is a fundamental part of ''security OF the cloud'', where AWS is responsible for the global infrastructure. Option A (incorrect) is a customer responsibility (ap-2.1-001) for ''security IN the cloud''. Option B (incorrect) is a customer responsibility (ap-2.1-004) for ''security IN the cloud''. Option D (incorrect) is a customer responsibility (ap-2.1-002) for IaaS services like Amazon EC2.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_58',
  'A company is using Amazon DynamoDB, a fully managed NoSQL database service. According to the AWS shared responsibility model, who is responsible for the patching of the underlying DynamoDB servers?',
  'mcq',
  '["The customer, as they own the data.","AWS, as it is a fully managed service.","A third-party security vendor.","The customer and AWS share this responsibility equally."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS, as it is a fully managed service. For managed services like Amazon DynamoDB, AWS is responsible for the ''security OF the cloud'', which includes managing and patching the underlying infrastructure, operating systems, and database software. This is a key distinction from IaaS services like EC2 (ap-2.1-003). Option A (incorrect) would be true for customer-managed databases on EC2, but not for DynamoDB. Option C (incorrect) is not the primary responsibility. Option D (incorrect) is too vague; for patching the underlying servers of a fully managed service, the responsibility lies squarely with AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_61_m_61_1_59',
  'Which statement accurately reflects the shared responsibility for network infrastructure in the AWS Cloud?',
  'mcq',
  '["The customer is responsible for managing all network devices and cabling within the AWS data centers.","AWS is responsible for the global network infrastructure, while the customer configures their virtual network within a VPC.","AWS and the customer jointly manage all aspects of the physical and virtual network infrastructure.","Network infrastructure is entirely the customer''s responsibility, including physical routing."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that AWS is responsible for the global network infrastructure, while the customer configures their virtual network within a VPC. AWS manages the ''security OF the cloud'', which includes the physical network components. The customer is responsible for ''security IN the cloud'', which involves configuring their virtual network components like Amazon VPCs, subnets, route tables, security groups, and Network ACLs. Option A (incorrect) is an AWS responsibility. Option C (incorrect) is too broad; while there''s shared responsibility, the specific division is clear. Option D (incorrect) is entirely false, as AWS manages the physical infrastructure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain61_13',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_61_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

