  '["Instance type and running hours","Storage capacity and data transfer","Number of requests and compute duration","Number of users and data ingress"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Number of requests and compute duration) are the primary factors for AWS Lambda pricing. You are charged based on the number of times your function is invoked (requests) and the duration (in milliseconds) it takes for your code to execute. Option A relates to Amazon EC2 pricing. Option B relates to storage services like Amazon S3. Option D is not a standard pricing model for Lambda.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_58',
  'Beyond the instance type, what is a key factor that influences the cost of an Amazon EC2 On-Demand instance?',
  'mcq',
  '["Number of S3 buckets","Data transfer out","Number of DynamoDB tables","AWS Support plan level"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (Data transfer out) is a significant factor influencing Amazon EC2 costs. While inbound data transfer is generally free, data transferred out from an EC2 instance to the internet or other AWS Regions is charged. Options A and C are unrelated services. Option D is a separate account-level cost, not directly tied to a single EC2 instance''s operational cost.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_59',
  'A development team is deploying a new web application that will run 24/7 for the next two years. To minimize the compute costs for the Amazon EC2 instances, which pricing model should they prioritize?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Spot Instances","Reserved Instances","AWS Free Tier"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Reserved Instances) are ideal for applications with steady-state, predictable usage running 24/7 for a long duration, offering significant cost savings over a 1- or 3-year commitment. Option A (On-Demand Instances) is more expensive for continuous workloads. Option B (Spot Instances) are interruptible and not suitable for a continuously running web application. Option D (AWS Free Tier) is for experimentation and has limited usage, not for production workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_60',
  'A data analytics team needs to process large datasets using Amazon EC2 instances. The processing jobs are fault-tolerant and can be interrupted and resumed. Which pricing model would be MOST cost-effective for these jobs?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Spot Instances","Dedicated Hosts"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Spot Instances) are the most cost-effective choice for fault-tolerant, interruptible workloads like batch data processing, offering substantial discounts compared to On-Demand pricing. Option A (On-Demand Instances) would be more expensive. Option B (Reserved Instances) are for continuous, predictable workloads, not interruptible ones. Option D (Dedicated Hosts) are for specific licensing needs and are generally more expensive.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_61',
  'A startup is experimenting with various AWS services to build a new application. They want to ensure they can explore and learn without incurring significant costs during the initial development phase. Which AWS offering helps them achieve this goal?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Reserved Instances","AWS Free Tier","AWS Spot Instances","AWS Savings Plans"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (AWS Free Tier) allows new AWS customers to use certain services up to a specific limit for free, either for 12 months, as an always-free offer, or as short-term trials. This is ideal for experimentation and learning without significant cost. Option A (AWS Reserved Instances) and Option D (AWS Savings Plans) offer significant discounts for committing to a consistent usage over a 1- or 3-year term, which is not suitable for initial experimentation. Option C (AWS Spot Instances) offers deep discounts for fault-tolerant, interruptible workloads, but it''s not primarily designed for free exploration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_62',
  'A company needs to process large volumes of data for batch analytics. The processing jobs can tolerate interruptions and have flexible start and end times. Which EC2 pricing model would be the MOST cost-effective for this workload?',
  'mcq',
  '["On-Demand Instances","Reserved Instances","Dedicated Hosts","Spot Instances"]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (Spot Instances) are the most cost-effective for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads like batch processing, offering up to a 90% discount compared to On-Demand prices. They can be interrupted by AWS with a two-minute notification. Option A (On-Demand Instances) are suitable for unpredictable workloads but are more expensive. Option B (Reserved Instances) are for steady-state, predictable workloads with a commitment. Option C (Dedicated Hosts) provide physical EC2 servers for license compliance and are generally the most expensive option.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_63',
  'Which statement accurately describes AWS data transfer pricing?',
  'mcq',
  '["All data transfer into AWS is charged, and all data transfer out of AWS is free.","Data transfer between AWS Regions is always free.","Inbound data transfer to AWS is generally free, while outbound data transfer from AWS is charged.","Data transfer within the same Availability Zone is always charged at a premium rate."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Inbound data transfer to AWS is generally free, while outbound data transfer from AWS is charged) accurately describes AWS data transfer pricing. Most data transferred into AWS services from the internet is free. Data transferred out of AWS to the internet, or between different AWS Regions or Availability Zones, typically incurs charges. Option A is incorrect as inbound is generally free and outbound is charged. Option B is incorrect as data transfer between AWS Regions is charged. Option D is incorrect as data transfer within the same Availability Zone is typically free or charged at a very low rate, not a premium.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_64',
  'What is the primary characteristic of On-Demand pricing for Amazon EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["It requires a 1- or 3-year commitment for discounted rates.","It allows bidding on unused capacity, with instances subject to interruption.","Customers pay for compute capacity by the second or hour with no long-term commitment.","It provides dedicated physical servers for specific licensing requirements."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Customers pay for compute capacity by the second or hour with no long-term commitment) is the primary characteristic of On-Demand pricing. It offers flexibility without upfront costs or long-term contracts. Option A describes Reserved Instances or Savings Plans. Option B describes Spot Instances. Option D describes Dedicated Hosts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_65',
  'A company is designing a new application that will use Amazon S3 for storage and AWS Lambda for serverless compute. How are these services typically priced?',
  'mcq',
  '["Both S3 and Lambda are priced solely based on instance hours.","S3 is priced by storage and requests, while Lambda is priced by invocations and duration.","Both S3 and Lambda require a 1-year upfront commitment.","S3 is priced by data transfer out only, and Lambda is always free."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (S3 is priced by storage and requests, while Lambda is priced by invocations and duration) accurately describes the pricing models. Amazon S3 charges for the amount of data stored, data transfer, and the number of requests made. AWS Lambda charges based on the number of invocations and the duration of each invocation (compute time). Option A is incorrect as instance hours apply to EC2, not S3 or Lambda. Option C is incorrect as neither S3 nor Lambda require a 1-year upfront commitment, though S3 has storage classes with minimum durations. Option D is incorrect as S3 charges for storage and requests, and Lambda has a free tier but is not always free.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_66',
  'Which of the following is a benefit of using AWS Savings Plans?',
  'mcq',
  '["They provide a fixed discount for specific EC2 instance types only.","They offer up to a 90% discount for interruptible workloads.","They provide flexibility to change instance family, operating system, or Region while maintaining a discount.","They are ideal for short-lived, unpredictable workloads with no commitment."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (They provide flexibility to change instance family, operating system, or Region while maintaining a discount) is a key benefit of Savings Plans. Unlike Reserved Instances, Savings Plans apply to usage across different instance families, operating systems, and Regions, providing more flexibility while still offering significant discounts for a commitment. Option A is incorrect because Savings Plans offer more flexibility than specific instance types. Option B describes Spot Instances. Option D describes On-Demand pricing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_67',
  'A company is migrating an existing application to AWS. They want to ensure that their development and testing environments, which are used intermittently, are as cost-effective as possible without requiring long-term commitments. Which pricing model is most suitable for these unpredictable workloads?',
  'mcq',
  '["Reserved Instances","Savings Plans","On-Demand pricing","Dedicated Hosts"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (On-Demand pricing) is the most suitable for unpredictable or short-lived workloads like development and testing environments, as it allows customers to pay for compute capacity by the second or hour with no long-term commitment. Option A (Reserved Instances) and Option B (Savings Plans) require a 1- or 3-year commitment and are best for steady-state workloads. Option D (Dedicated Hosts) are for specific licensing requirements and are generally the most expensive.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_68',
  'What is a key characteristic of the AWS Free Tier ''12 months free'' offer?',
  'mcq',
  '["It applies to all AWS services indefinitely.","It provides unlimited usage for the first year.","It is available only to existing AWS customers.","It offers specific usage limits for certain services for the first 12 months after account creation."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (It offers specific usage limits for certain services for the first 12 months after account creation) accurately describes the ''12 months free'' offer. It provides limited free usage for services like Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, and Amazon RDS for the first year. Option A is incorrect as it applies to specific services and limits, not all indefinitely. Option B is incorrect as usage is limited, not unlimited. Option C is incorrect as it''s primarily for new AWS customers.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_69',
  'When estimating the total cost of ownership for a data-intensive application on AWS, which cost factor is often overlooked but can significantly impact the bill?',
  'mcq',
  '["Compute instance type selection","Storage class of Amazon S3","Data transfer costs, especially outbound and cross-Region","Number of Amazon SQS queues used"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (Data transfer costs, especially outbound and cross-Region) is a common cost factor that is often overlooked but can significantly impact the total bill for data-intensive applications. While inbound data transfer is mostly free, outbound data transfer to the internet and data transfer between different AWS Regions or Availability Zones are charged. Option A (Compute instance type selection) and Option B (Storage class of Amazon S3) are typically considered during cost estimation. Option D (Number of Amazon SQS queues used) is generally a minor cost factor compared to data transfer for data-intensive applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_70',
  'A media company is processing large volumes of video files for transcoding. These jobs are fault-tolerant, can be interrupted, and do not have strict completion deadlines. The company needs to minimize compute costs significantly. Which Amazon EC2 pricing model should be recommended for these workloads to achieve the MOST cost-effective solution?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, offering up to a 90% discount compared to On-Demand pricing, suitable for interruptible workloads.","Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, providing significant discounts for a 1-year or 3-year commitment to consistent usage patterns.","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, allowing payment for compute capacity by the second with no long-term commitments or upfront costs.","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, offering physical EC2 server capacity dedicated to your use, which can be useful for specific licensing requirements."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon EC2 Spot Instances. Spot Instances allow users to bid on unused EC2 capacity, offering up to a 90% discount compared to On-Demand pricing. They are ideal for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads like video transcoding that can tolerate interruptions, making them the most cost-effective choice for this scenario. Option B (incorrect) refers to Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, which provide significant discounts for predictable, steady-state workloads with a 1-year or 3-year commitment. They are not suitable for interruptible jobs where the instance might be reclaimed. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, which offer flexibility with no upfront commitment but are the most expensive pricing option, making them suboptimal for cost minimization in this scenario. Option D (incorrect) mentions Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, which provide dedicated physical servers. While they offer specific licensing benefits, they are generally the most expensive option and do not align with the goal of minimizing compute costs for interruptible tasks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_71',
  'A startup is launching a new web application with a highly predictable, steady-state workload that is expected to run 24/7 for the next three years. They want to ensure the MOST cost-effective compute solution while maintaining consistent performance. Which Amazon EC2 pricing model should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Spot Instances to leverage unused capacity for maximum savings, accepting potential interruptions.","Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances or Savings Plans, committing to a 1-year or 3-year term for significant discounts on predictable usage.","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, providing flexibility to scale up or down without any long-term commitments.","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, offering dedicated physical servers for specific compliance or licensing requirements."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances or Savings Plans. For predictable, steady-state workloads running for an extended period (like three years), committing to a 1-year or 3-year term through Reserved Instances or Savings Plans offers significant discounts (up to 72% compared to On-Demand). This makes them the most cost-effective choice for consistent usage. Option A (incorrect) refers to Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, which are highly cost-effective but are designed for fault-tolerant, interruptible workloads, not for steady-state applications that require continuous availability. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, which offer maximum flexibility but are the most expensive pricing option, making them unsuitable for long-term cost optimization. Option D (incorrect) mentions Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, which provide dedicated physical servers and are generally the most expensive option, not aligned with general cost-effectiveness for a typical web application.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_72',
  'A company is designing a new application that will transfer large amounts of data between Amazon EC2 instances located in different AWS Regions. They are concerned about the potential impact on their AWS bill. Which statement accurately describes how data transfer costs are typically applied in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["All data transfer into and out of AWS Regions is free, regardless of the volume.","Data transfer between AWS Regions is generally free, but data transfer within the same Region is charged.","Data transfer inbound to AWS is free, while data transfer outbound from AWS and between different AWS Regions is charged.","Data transfer is only charged when moving data between different AWS accounts, not within the same account."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) accurately states that data transfer inbound to AWS is generally free, while data transfer outbound from AWS and between different AWS Regions is charged. This is a fundamental aspect of AWS pricing. Option A (incorrect) is false; data transfer out of AWS and between Regions is charged. Option B (incorrect) is also false; data transfer within the same Region (e.g., within the same Availability Zone or between Availability Zones in the same Region) can incur charges, and data transfer between Regions is definitely charged. Option D (incorrect) is incorrect as data transfer costs apply based on direction and location, not solely on whether it''s between different AWS accounts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_73',
  'A new developer is exploring AWS services for personal learning and experimentation. They want to minimize costs while gaining hands-on experience with services like Amazon EC2, Amazon S3, and AWS Lambda. Which AWS offering allows them to use a limited amount of these services without incurring charges for a specific period?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Enterprise Support, which includes a dedicated Technical Account Manager and cost optimization guidance.","AWS Savings Plans, providing discounts for committing to a consistent amount of compute usage over 1 or 3 years.","The AWS Free Tier, offering certain services for free up to specific usage limits for 12 months, or as ''always free'' for some services.","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, which provide significant discounts on unused EC2 capacity but are subject to interruption."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is the AWS Free Tier. The AWS Free Tier allows new AWS customers to use certain AWS services for free up to specific usage limits for 12 months following their AWS account sign-up date. It also includes ''always free'' offers and short-term trials for other services, making it ideal for learning and experimentation without incurring charges. Option A (incorrect) is AWS Enterprise Support, which is a paid support plan designed for large enterprises and does not offer free service usage. Option B (incorrect) refers to AWS Savings Plans, which are a pricing model for committed usage and require a financial commitment, not free usage. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, which offer discounts but are a paid service and are not part of a free learning program.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_74',
  'A development team is building a new application with highly unpredictable and spiky usage patterns. They need to ensure that their compute resources can scale up and down rapidly without any long-term commitments or upfront payments. Which Amazon EC2 pricing model provides the MOST flexibility for this type of workload?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, allowing payment by the second for compute capacity without commitments.","Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, offering significant discounts for consistent usage over a 1-year or 3-year term.","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, providing deep discounts for fault-tolerant workloads that can tolerate interruptions.","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, which are physical servers dedicated to a single customer for specific licensing needs."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances. On-Demand pricing allows users to pay for compute capacity by the second (or hour for older instances) with no long-term commitments. This model provides the highest flexibility to scale resources up or down rapidly in response to unpredictable and spiky workloads, making it ideal when flexibility is paramount. Option B (incorrect) refers to Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, which are designed for predictable, steady-state workloads and require a commitment, making them unsuitable for highly variable usage. Option C (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, which offer significant cost savings but are subject to interruption, making them unsuitable for workloads that cannot tolerate sudden termination. Option D (incorrect) mentions Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, which provide dedicated physical servers and are generally the most expensive option, not aligned with the need for flexible, cost-effective scaling for unpredictable workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_75',
  'A company stores petabytes of archival data in Amazon S3 that is rarely accessed but must be retained for compliance. They want to understand the primary cost components associated with using Amazon S3 for this purpose. Which two factors are the main drivers of Amazon S3 costs?',
  'mcq',
  '["The number of EC2 instances used to access the data and the data transfer out to the internet.","The amount of data stored and the number of requests made to the data.","The number of Amazon CloudFront distributions used and the geographic location of the S3 bucket.","The duration the data is stored and the type of encryption applied to the objects."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately identifies the amount of data stored and the number of requests made to the data as the primary cost components for Amazon S3. S3 pricing is fundamentally based on storage consumed (per GB-month) and the number of requests (GET, PUT, LIST, etc.). Option A (incorrect) involves Amazon EC2 and data transfer out, which are separate cost considerations, not primary S3 storage costs. Option C (incorrect) mentions Amazon CloudFront, a CDN service, and geographic location, which influences pricing but isn''t a primary cost driver like storage and requests. Option D (incorrect) states duration and encryption type; while duration is inherent in storage cost calculation (per GB-month), encryption type itself doesn''t directly drive S3 costs in the same way as storage volume and requests.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_76',
  'A development team is building a serverless application using AWS Lambda. They want to understand how the costs for their Lambda functions are primarily calculated. Which two factors are the main drivers of AWS Lambda costs?',
  'mcq',
  '["The number of API Gateway requests and the amount of data transferred out of the Lambda function.","The number of invocations of the function and the duration (execution time) of each invocation.","The amount of storage consumed by the Lambda function code and the number of concurrent executions.","The type of EC2 instance used for the Lambda execution environment and the network bandwidth utilized."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately identifies the number of invocations and the duration (execution time) of each invocation as the primary drivers of AWS Lambda costs. Lambda pricing is based on the number of times a function is invoked and the total compute time consumed across all invocations, measured in milliseconds, along with the memory allocated to the function. Option A (incorrect) mentions API Gateway requests and data transfer out, which are separate service costs, not primary Lambda function costs. Option C (incorrect) refers to storage consumed by code (which is minimal and often covered by free tier) and concurrent executions (which impact performance and scaling but are not the direct pricing model components like invocations and duration). Option D (incorrect) is incorrect because AWS Lambda is a serverless service, meaning users do not manage or select EC2 instance types, and network bandwidth is typically included in the duration cost.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_77',
  'A data analytics company frequently runs large, fault-tolerant batch processing jobs that can be interrupted and restarted without issues. They are looking for the MOST cost-effective way to provision compute resources for these jobs. Which Amazon EC2 pricing model should they prioritize?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, committing to a 1-year term for a fixed discount.","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, paying for capacity by the second without any commitments.","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, bidding on unused EC2 capacity for significant cost savings.","Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, providing dedicated physical servers for maximum control."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is Amazon EC2 Spot Instances. Spot Instances are designed for fault-tolerant, flexible workloads that can tolerate interruptions, such as batch processing, data analysis, or CI/CD jobs. They offer up to a 90% discount compared to On-Demand pricing, making them the most cost-effective solution for this specific use case. Option A (incorrect) refers to Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, which are best for predictable, steady-state workloads that run continuously, not for interruptible batch jobs. Option B (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, which offer flexibility but are the most expensive option and do not provide the significant cost savings needed for highly cost-sensitive batch processing. Option D (incorrect) mentions Amazon EC2 Dedicated Hosts, which are the most expensive option and are used for specific licensing or compliance requirements, not for general cost-effective batch processing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_78',
  'A global company operates a data processing pipeline that transfers large volumes of processed data from an Amazon S3 bucket in us-east-1 to an Amazon EC2 instance in eu-west-1 for further analysis. The company wants to optimize its AWS bill. Which action would MOST directly help reduce data transfer costs in this scenario?',
  'mcq',
  '["Configuring a VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in us-east-1 to keep traffic within the AWS network.","Using Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive for storing the data in us-east-1 to reduce storage costs.","Processing the data using an Amazon EC2 instance located in the same us-east-1 Region as the S3 bucket.","Implementing AWS Budgets to set alerts for high data transfer costs."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) would most directly help reduce data transfer costs. Data transfer between different AWS Regions (e.g., us-east-1 to eu-west-1) incurs significant charges. By processing the data using an Amazon EC2 instance located in the same us-east-1 Region as the Amazon S3 bucket, the cross-Region data transfer charges are avoided, as data transfer within the same Region or Availability Zone is either free or significantly cheaper. Option A (incorrect) refers to VPC endpoints, which are useful for keeping traffic within the AWS network and avoiding internet charges, but they do not eliminate cross-Region data transfer costs. Option B (incorrect) suggests using Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive, which reduces storage costs but does not impact data transfer costs. Option D (incorrect) involves AWS Budgets, which helps monitor and alert on costs but does not inherently reduce the actual data transfer charges.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_63_m_63_1_79',
  'A software development team needs to provision a new Amazon EC2 instance for a short-term project that will last approximately two months. The workload is not critical and can tolerate some downtime for maintenance. The primary goal is to minimize upfront costs and maintain flexibility. Which EC2 pricing model is best suited for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, purchased for a 1-year term to secure a discount.","Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances, paying only for the compute capacity used by the second.","Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, bidding on unused capacity for significant savings.","Amazon EC2 Savings Plans, committing to a consistent spend for compute services over 1 or 3 years."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances. For a short-term project (two months) with a need for flexibility and minimal upfront costs, On-Demand Instances are ideal. You pay only for the compute capacity you use by the second, with no long-term commitments or upfront payments. Option A (incorrect) refers to Amazon EC2 Reserved Instances, which require a 1-year or 3-year commitment and are not suitable for a two-month project, as the commitment would extend far beyond the project duration. Option C (incorrect) mentions Amazon EC2 Spot Instances, which offer significant savings but are subject to interruption, and while the workload can tolerate some downtime, On-Demand provides more predictable availability for a short-term project where the primary goal is flexibility and no commitment. Option D (incorrect) describes Amazon EC2 Savings Plans, which also require a 1-year or 3-year commitment and are not appropriate for a short-term project.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain63_13',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_63_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

